1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Hi, in this video we're going to look at generating a CSERF proof of concept with Burp Suite.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Cross-site request forgery, or CSERF attacks, force a victim user to perform unintended actions on a website.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:24,000
If a website is vulnerable to CSERF, an attacker can bypass the same origin policy,

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:37,000
which usually prevents websites from interfering with each other, meaning the attacker can create a web page that causes the victim user's browser to submit a request directly to the vulnerable website.

5
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
You can use Burp Suite Professional to automatically generate HTML for a proof of concept CSERF attack.

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,000
This is quicker and easier than manually creating the HTML, which can be cumbersome when the request contains a larger number of parameters.

7
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,000
You can follow along using the lab CSERF Vulnerability with No Defenses in our Web Security Academy.

8
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Once the lab has launched, log in with the user credentials from the lab solution and identify a request you think may be vulnerable to CSERF.

9
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
You can use Burp Scanner to identify requests that are potentially vulnerable.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
In this case, we're going to use the My Account Change Email request.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Right-click the request and select Engagement Tools Generate CSERF POC.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,000
A dialogue opens with the HTML based on the selected request.

13
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
In the HTML, you can edit the values in the fields you want to change in the proof of concept attack.

14
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
We're just going to change the email address here.

15
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Click Copy HTML to copy the HTML to your clipboard.

16
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
And then paste the HTML into a web page.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
If you're using the lab, paste the HTML into the exploit server.

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
View the web page in a browser that's logged into the vulnerable website.

19
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,000
So in the lab, we can click on View Exploit from the browser where you logged into the application.

20
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Review the response in your browser and Burp's proxy HTTP history tab to see whether the desired action has occurred.

21
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Here we can see the user's email address has been updated.

22
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
To verify the vulnerability, log in to the application with a different account.

23
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Repeat the attack with the same HTML.

24
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
A successful attack confirms that the application is vulnerable to CSERF.

25
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,000
If you're using the lab, we're just going to update the email address and click on Deliver to Victim.

26
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Click on Deliver to Victim.

